result: 'Delivered docs/idiomatic.md (Part 1: evidence-backed anti-pattern catalog
  with remediations, severity tiers S1-S3, grounded in real file:line citations) and
  docs/fmt.md (Part 2: jinn fmt + jinn lint technical spec). Spec covers: layered
  architecture reusing the jinnc frontend, lossless CST/trivia (fixing the existing
  comment-eating reprinter in src/fmt.rs), a 4-tier rule engine (T1 lexical / T2 flow-shape
  / T3 type-aware / T4 lint) with stable rule ids, configuration, CLI (--check/--diff/--stdin/--type-aware/--fix),
  a verified behavior-preservation + idempotency safety model, bug lints (incl. the
  is/equals confusion J3105), a testing strategy mirroring conformance suites, and
  a phased rollout.'
description: 'Two-part deliverable:

  PART 1: Research the existing Jinn code corpus (tests, snippets/, apps/, benchmarks/,
  std/, libjn/, conformance suites) to catalog anti-patterns, unsightly idioms, ugly
  syntax, and non-idiomatic constructs. For each, document why it''s bad and propose
  an idiomatic remediation that leverages inference, implicit declarations, and the
  intelligent compiler. Output: a syntax refinement / course-correction document.

  PART 2: Design a technical spec for `jinn fmt` — a formatter like rustfmt but more
  powerful, that rewrites un-idiomatic / smelly Jinn into clean idiomatic Jinn with
  NO behavior change, plus an integrated lint (`jinn lint`) that finds bugs and syntax
  issues. Spec should cover architecture (reuse compiler frontend: lexer/parser/HIR),
  rule taxonomy, fix/rewrite engine, configuration, CLI, idempotency/safety guarantees,
  and integration with jinnc.

  Definition of done: docs/fmt.md (the spec) and docs/idiomatic.md (Part 1 refinement
  catalog) written, grounded in real examples from the codebase with file:line citations.'
